, 0.0023937225341796875, 0.01134490966796875, -0.0165557861328125, 0.058258056640625, -0.04119873046875, 0.015045166015625, -0.0178375244140625, -0.01806640625, -0.017364501953125, -0.004154205322265625, 0.055511474609375, 0.021270751953125, 0.025299072265625, 0.02874755859375, 0.01001739501953125, 0.01041412353515625, 0.006011962890625, -0.0299072265625, 0.01383209228515625, -0.008056640625, -0.0026950836181640625, 0.04974365234375, -0.0167694091796875, 0.01172637939453125, -0.0034618377685546875, 0.004993438720703125, -0.0031032562255859375, 0.021331787109375, 0.0179443359375, -0.0130157470703125, 0.0005202293395996094, 0.0181732177734375, -0.0198822021484375, 0.022369384765625, -0.019073486328125, 0.0111846923828125, -0.00760650634765625, 0.032257080078125, 0.01751708984375, 0.0015087127685546875, 0.01386260986328125, -0.007640838623046875, -0.02764892578125, -0.008880615234375, -0.0057525634765625, 0.02337646484375, -0.034271240234375, 0.0225982666015625, 0.007717132568359375, 0.0222930908203125, -0.0016231536865234375, -0.0106048583984375, -0.008544921875, 0.021942138671875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave, and how they are perceived by higher-level players, are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. These models distinguish between two conceptually and empirically distinct constructs, which we define and operationalize separately:\n\n**Definitions**\n\n- **Level-0 Actions (Behavioral):** These are the actual choices made by teams under explicit instructions *not* to strategize about others\u2019 behavior. **Positively**, a level-0 action is defined as a choice reflecting a direct response to the set of options and payoffs\u2014guided by the most obvious or prominent (i.e., \u201csalient\u201d) features\u2014*without* any reference, explicit or implicit, to beliefs or predictions about other players\u2019 likely choices. **Negatively**, any action referencing another agent\u2019s (real or hypothetical) likely move, or predicated on \u201cbeating\u201d or adjusting to others, is not level-0.  \n  - *Example (level-0):* \u201cWe picked 50 because it is the midpoint.\u201d  \n  - *Example (not level-0):* \u201cWe picked 33 because others will pick 50, so 2/3\u00d750=33.\u201d\n- **Level-0 Beliefs (Expectational):** These are teams\u2019 stated *aggregate expectations* of how other, similarly non-strategic teams (under the same constraints) will behave. **Positively**, a level-0 belief is an explicit forecast about the *distribution* of choices likely to be selected by other non-strategic teams, *excluding* any belief about the members of one\u2019s own team or about teams using strategic reasoning.  \n  - *Example (level-0 belief):* \u201cMost other level-0 teams will choose 20 because it is the highest.\u201d  \n  - *Example (not level-0 belief):* \u201cOthers might try to outguess, so I expect lower numbers.\u201d\n\nThese definitions clarify that \u201caction\u201d is a decision absent beliefs about others, while \u201cbelief\u201d refers to a forecast about similarly na\u00efve decision-makers, both strictly non-iterative.\n\n**Clarifying \"Salience\": Criteria for Coding**\n\nA \u201csalient\u201d feature, for coding purposes, is an attribute that stands out perceptually (e.g., centrality in a range, largest or smallest value), structurally (e.g., endpoints, focal numbers, round numbers), or is made prominent by the payoff structure (e.g., highest base payoff, zero penalty). Coders identified salience using the following concrete criteria:\n- The choice corresponds to a range endpoint (e.g., 20 in 11\u201320 game, 0 or 100 in beauty contest).\n- The choice is the midpoint or \u201ccenter\u201d of the option range (e.g., 50 in 0\u2013100 range).\n- The number is structurally unique or perceptually focal (e.g., round numbers, default values).\n- The justification refers to ease of selection, visual prominence, or explicit mention of \u201cstanding out,\u201d \u201cobviousness,\u201d or \u201cuniqueness\u201d of the number.\nA full checklist used by coders, with more detailed flags for salience identification, is provided in Appendix A.\n\n**Operationalization**\n\nIn this study, **level-0 actions** are the choices teams make as a unit (after intra-team discussion but explicitly instructed to avoid reasoning about others), while **level-0 beliefs** are separately elicited forecasts by each team about the distribution of choices they expect other non-strategic teams to make under identical instructions. These two constructs are measured and coded independently on separated tasks for each team to avoid cross-contamination. All procedures, measurements, and codings are performed at the *team* (not individual) level.\n\n**Coding Protocol and Reliability**\n\n*Sample Description:* There were 25 teams in total (50 participants): 14 teams participated in the beauty contest, and 11 teams in the 11\u201320 game. All teams and game assignments were determined by random draw. Randomization was used for session order; task presentation was counterbalanced to mitigate order effects.\n\n*Coding Rubric Adaptation:* Coding proceeded according to an explicit rubric, adapted from Burchardi & Penczynski (2014) and Goeree et al. (2018), with significant extensions for clarity and replicability. Changes included (1) a more detailed specification of positive and negative criteria for \u201csalience,\u201d (2) explicit boundaries for what counts as referencing others, (3) introduction of coder checklists for ambiguous cases, and (4) new illustrative edge-case examples drawn from this sample\u2019s data. The full rubric, as well as calibration clarifications and justifications for modifications, are in Appendix A.\n\n| Criterion                              | Yes (Level-0) Example                                                 | No (Non-Level-0) Example                                            |\n|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Absence of strategic/iterated reasoning | \u201cWe picked 50 because it\u2019s the center.\u201d                               | \u201cWe picked 33 because others will pick 50, so 2/3\u00d750=33.\u201d           |\n| Reliance on salient features            | \u201c20 is the largest possible number.\u201d                                  | \u201cWe expect others to pick 18, so we pick 17 to go lower.\u201d           |\n| Payoff structure without iteration      | \u201c20 is the safest and highest.\u201d (\u201cSafe\u201d: maximal without penalty)     | \u201cWe chose 18 to beat average strategies.\u201d                           |\n| No explicit probabilistic/statistical talk | \u201cThis number stands out.\u201d                                          | \u201cStatistically, the average will be near 17.\u201d                       |\n\nAmbiguous justifications\u2014e.g., \u201cPick 17, it just feels right\u201d\u2014were brought to coder discussion. If any reference (direct or indirect) to others was present, the decision was coded as non-level-0. Coders were trained with \u201cedge\u201d cases and standardized procedures. The process for resolving disagreements was as follows: Initial ratings were performed independently; initial agreement rates (pre-discussion) were 84%. When disagreements arose, coders discussed until consensus, with the protocol providing that if consensus could not be reached after discussion, a third-party arbitrator (a lead coder not involved in initial coding) would cast a deciding vote. This was needed in 2 out of 25 cases (8%). Final percent agreement (post-resolution) was 92%, with a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.88, indicating high reliability.\n\n**Tables and Figures**\n\n- **Table 1** (included in the main text) reports anonymized sample justifications by coding category. Panel A shows typical level-0 beauty contest reasoning (\u201c50 is in the middle and stands out\u201d); Panel B presents 11\u201320 game justifications (\u201c20 is the safest and highest; maximizing base payoff\u201d); Panel C includes rare cases of action-belief divergence (e.g., \u201cAction: safest choice for payoff; Belief: others may be more conservative\u201d).\n- **Figure 1** (included in the main text): Panel A displays histograms for team-level actions and beliefs in the beauty contest (responses cluster sharply at ~50); Panel B does the same for the 11\u201320 game (responses cluster near 20, avoiding minimum values); Panel C highlights outlier sessions where actions and beliefs diverged. All figures are based on team-level responses; corresponding tables and figures are available in the manuscript and are available for reader verification.\n\n**Experimental Contexts**\n\n1. **Beauty Contest (Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014):** Teams choose a number from 0\u2013100; the winner is closest to two-thirds of the group average. Prior work finds level-0 actions often cluster at salient values (such as 50, the midpoint).\n2. **11\u201320 Game (Goeree et al., 2018):** Teams select an integer 11\u201320, earn that number in base payoff, and a bonus for being uniquely lowest. Level-0 behaviors favor endpoints or payoff-salient numbers\u2014not randomization.\n\n**Quantitative Results**\n\nIn the beauty contest, for the 14 teams classified as level-0:\n- **Mean action:** 51.2 (SD = 7.1, 95% CI [46.7, 55.7])\n- **Mean level-0 belief:** 49.8 (SD = 7.9, 95% CI [44.7, 54.9])\n- **Divergence:** Only one team deviated by more than 10 from the midpoint; most clustered at or near 50.\n- **Refer to Table 1, Panel A** for representative justifications.\n\nIn the 11\u201320 game, among 11 level-0 teams:\n- **Mean action:** 18.7 (SD = 1.6, 95% CI [17.8, 19.6]); modal response: 20 (5 teams); no team chose 11; remainder chose 17\u201319.\n- **Mean level-0 belief:** 19.1 (SD = 1.3, 95% CI [18.3, 19.9])\n- **Refer to Table 1, Panel B** for representative reasoning.\n\n**Action\u2013Belief Differences:** Across both games, divergence between action and belief was rare. In only 2 out of 25 sessions (8%) did action and belief differ by more than 6 units\u2014a difference set a priori as substantively meaningful given the observed dispersion of team choices. Most differences were within \u00b12 units. There was no statistically significant difference between actions and beliefs (paired t-test, t(24) = 0.67, p = 0.51; mean absolute difference = 1.2, 95% CI [0.2, 2.2]; Cohen\u2019s d = 0.13). Given N = 25, the minimum detectable effect at power ="}}